### 1.1 Camera & Presentation

Each level is presented as a single contained playfield sized to fit the device's viewport at a fixed world-to-screen scale, in the tradition of Fireboy & Watergirl. Larger levels (introduced from World 2 onward) use a smooth-follow camera with soft bounds, easing over 0.15s, to avoid disorienting the player during rapid element swaps. The camera never cuts or hard-snaps during a transformation — the swap is a character-local event, not a camera event, which keeps the player spatially oriented.


## 2. Core Game Concept

Element Shift reimagines the Fireboy & Watergirl puzzle-platformer format around a single protagonist instead of a co-op pair. Instead of switching control between two characters, the player instantly reshapes one character's elemental nature. This preserves the genre's signature "right tool, right moment" puzzle logic while removing split-attention control and making the game natively single-player and mobile-friendly.


### 2.1 The Transformation Fantasy

The transformation is the game's central verb and its marketing hook. It is designed to feel like two established touchstones:

| Reference | What We Borrow |
| --- | --- |
| Ben 10 (Omnitrix) | The idea of a compact selector (wheel/dial) that instantly commits to a new form, with a signature flash/silhouette-change animation and a short recognizable transformation stinger sound |
| GTA V (character switch) | The responsiveness and lack of input lag — the player never feels like they are waiting on the game; the switch reads as an interrupt, not a queued animation |


### 2.2 Transformation Specification

| Property | Target Value | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Total swap duration | 0.20–0.30s | From input to full control restored |
| Input buffering | Movement/jump inputs during swap are buffered, not dropped | Prevents the swap from feeling like a stall |
| Visual feedback | Radial particle burst in the new element's color + 3-frame silhouette morph | Readable at small mobile sizes (see Visual Identity §8) |
| Audio feedback | One-shot transformation stinger, element-specific (see Audio Design §9) | < 300ms so it never lags behind the animation |
| Cooldown | None by default; some puzzle types may impose a short (0.5–1s) cooldown as an explicit obstacle, never as a baseline restriction | Keeps the core loop friction-free |
| Mid-air swaps | Allowed unless a specific level/puzzle explicitly disables it (rare, telegraphed obstacle) | Needed for combo puzzles like Wood-bridge-then-Fire-dash |


### 2.3 Design Pillars Restated as Player Promises

  - "I can always act." — No dead time waiting on animations or menus.
  - "I can always tell what I am." — Silhouette, color, and particle language are unambiguous at a glance.
  - "Every element matters." — No element is strictly dominant; each level is tuned so at least two forms are required.
  - "Failure is cheap." — Instant respawn at the last safe point, no lives system, no punishing backtracking.


## 3. Core Elements

Each element is a complete traversal-and-interaction kit. Elements are introduced one at a time across the first quarter of World 1 so players build a solid mental model before combinations are required (see Level Design Philosophy §5).


### 3.1 Fire

Identity: aggressive, quick, destructive. Fire clears obstacles and lights the way; it is the "offense" element.

| Category | Details |
| --- | --- |
| Abilities | Burn vines and foliage; ignite mechanisms (torches, fuses, fire switches); melt ice blocks and frozen platforms; activate fire-only switches and gates |
| Traversal | Standard run/jump; slightly faster ground speed (+10%) to sell the "quick" identity |
| Weaknesses | Extinguished instantly by water hazards (pools, waterfalls, water-switch traps); cannot cross extinguishing zones (mist vents, wet moss) without being forced back to Water or losing the form's active effects |
| Signature interaction | Standing near flammable terrain (vines, dry wood, fuses) auto-highlights it with a warm glow when Fire is active, telegraphing the intended solution |
| Color language | Warm red-orange (#C0392B core, #FF8A50 particle highlight) |


### 3.2 Water

Identity: fluid, defensive, enabling. Water is the "utility" element — it opens paths Fire and Earth cannot survive.

| Category | Details |
| --- | --- |
| Abilities | Swim freely through water volumes (no drowning, controlled buoyancy); extinguish fire hazards and fire-only gates on contact; activate water-only mechanisms (valves, turbines, pressure locks) |
| Traversal | Reduced gravity and controlled sink/rise while submerged; normal platforming on land, slightly reduced ground friction ("slippery" feel) |
| Weaknesses | Instantly incapacitated (brief stun + knockback) by electrified water hazards; frozen solid (temporary immobilize) if it enters a frozen-area trap without first being thawed |
| Signature interaction | Water volumes render with a light ripple/caustic effect only when the character can currently interact with them, reinforcing "this is for me" |
| Color language | Cool blue (#1F6FB2 core, #7FD1E8 particle highlight) |


### 3.3 Earth

Identity: strong, deliberate, structural. Earth is the "heavy" element — it moves mass and takes hits.

| Category | Details |
| --- | --- |
| Abilities | Push and pull heavy crates/boulders; summon temporary stone platforms at marked anchor points; activate pressure switches by standing on them (extra weight class vs. other forms); break fragile/cracked floors to reveal lower routes |
| Traversal | Slower ground speed (−15%), higher jump but with more "weight" in the jump arc; cannot be knocked back by minor hazards |
| Weaknesses | Crumbling surfaces collapse under Earth's weight after a short delay, so Earth cannot linger on marked cracked terrain; cannot swim (sinks) and cannot cross deep water |
| Signature interaction | Pressure switches are color-coded and only depress fully under Earth's weight, visually distinguishing them from universal switches |
| Color language | Warm brown/ochre (#8A5A32 core, #D9A15B particle highlight) |


### 3.4 Wood

Identity: flexible, life-giving, connective. Wood is the "structural utility" element — it bridges gaps in space and time.

| Category | Details |
| --- | --- |
| Abilities | Extend the body into a temporary rigid bridge across gaps (timed or held); grow vines from seed anchors to create climbable routes; spawn temporary wooden platforms/planks that decay after a set duration; interact with seed pods and nature-triggered objects (pressure-free growth switches) |
| Traversal | Normal ground speed; bridge/vine states lock the character in place while active (channelled ability, not a movement mode) |
| Weaknesses | Fire instantly destroys any active Wood structure (bridge, vine, platform) including ones the player is standing on, and Wood itself takes damage from open flame contact |
| Signature interaction | Growth seed anchors pulse with a soft green glow only when Wood is the active form, and show a countdown shimmer just before a grown structure decays |
| Color language | Natural green (#3D7A3D core, #8FD17A particle highlight) |


### 3.5 Element Comparison Matrix

| Element | Strong vs. | Weak vs. | Primary Verb | Speed | Special Trait |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Fire | Vines, ice, fuses | Water, mist | Ignite / Melt | Fast (+10%) | Destroys Wood structures on contact |
| Water | Fire, water gates | Electricity, frozen traps | Extinguish / Swim | Normal (slippery) | Only form that swims freely |
| Earth | Crates, pressure switches, fragile floors | Crumbling ground, deep water | Push / Press / Break | Slow (−15%) | Only form pressure switches fully register |
| Wood | Gaps, seed switches | Fire | Bridge / Grow | Normal | Only form that creates temporary structures |

