## 4. Gameplay Pillars

Every mechanic, level, and monetization decision is validated against these six pillars. If a proposed feature does not clearly serve at least one pillar, it is cut.

| # | Pillar | What It Means In Practice |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Instant Transformation | Swaps are sub-0.3s, input-buffered, and never gate the player behind a menu or animation lock. |
| 2 | Environmental Puzzle Solving | Every obstacle has a legible elemental solution; puzzles are read, not guessed. |
| 3 | Combination of Multiple Elements | From World 2 onward, most puzzles require sequencing 2+ elements, not just picking the "correct" one. |
| 4 | Progressive Mechanic Introduction | New verbs are taught one at a time in a safe sandbox before being combined or tested under pressure. |
| 5 | Short Satisfying Levels | 60–180s per level; every level ends on a clear, readable success state (goal reached). |
| 6 | Mobile-Friendly Controls | Playable one-handed in portrait or two-handed in landscape, with all interactive elements reachable by thumb. |


## 5. Mobile Controls


### 5.1 Control Scheme Overview

Orientation: landscape (primary), to maximize horizontal puzzle visibility and match the genre convention. The layout below assumes landscape; a portrait fallback is described in 5.5.

| Input | Control | Placement |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Move Left/Right | Virtual directional pad (thumb-drag zone, not a fixed joystick graphic) or on-screen left/right buttons — configurable in Settings | Bottom-left, thumb reach zone |
| Jump | Single tap button | Bottom-right, thumb reach zone |
| Element Swap (quick) | 4 quick-swap buttons arranged in a fan/arc above the jump button | Bottom-right cluster |
| Element Swap (wheel, optional) | Long-press the currently active element button to open a radial wheel; drag to select, release to confirm | Overlay, centered on the long-pressed button |
| Pause / Menu | Top-right corner icon | Top-right, out of the active play zone |


### 5.2 Quick-Swap vs. Element Wheel

We specify two selection methods because mobile puzzle-platformers must support both twitch play and deliberate play:

  - Quick-Swap Buttons (default, recommended primary interface): four fixed, always-visible buttons, one per element, color-coded and iconified. A single tap instantly commits to that element. This best serves Pillar 1 (Instant Transformation) because there is zero selection ambiguity or drag distance.
  - Element Wheel (secondary, optional accessibility/preference mode): a radial menu invoked by long-press, useful for players who prefer a single larger input target or who play one-handed with a thumb resting near one corner. Selection still commits on release, and total time from press to confirm must stay within the 0.2–0.3s transformation budget once the player is familiar with the wheel.

Default recommendation: ship with Quick-Swap Buttons as the default control scheme, and expose the Element Wheel as a toggle in Settings > Controls. Playtesting during the prototype phase (see Roadmap) should validate which scheme the majority of testers prefer as default.


### 5.3 One-Handed vs. Two-Handed Usability

| Mode | Feasibility | Design Accommodation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Two-handed landscape (primary target) | Fully supported | Left thumb on movement, right thumb on jump/swap cluster; this is the intended default experience |
| One-handed landscape | Supported via Element Wheel + edge-anchored movement | Movement pad can be dragged to either screen edge in Settings; Element Wheel reduces required reach to a single long-press zone |
| Portrait one-handed | Supported for casual/menu play, not recommended for precision levels | See 5.5 portrait fallback layout |


### 5.4 Accessibility Considerations

  - Adjustable button size and opacity (Settings > Controls > HUD Scale).
  - Colorblind-safe element iconography: each element uses a distinct icon shape (flame, droplet, rock, leaf) in addition to color, never color alone.
  - Haptic feedback toggle for transformation and hazard-death events (on by default on supported devices).
  - Adjustable input deadzone and button remapping (swap left/right cluster, move jump button).
  - "Hold to confirm" alternative for the Element Wheel to reduce accidental swaps for players with motor-control needs.
  - No time-limited inputs required to progress the main story path (time trials are optional side objectives only, see §7).


### 5.5 Portrait Fallback Layout

Portrait mode is supported for menus, level select, and casual play, using a compressed HUD: movement pad bottom-left, jump + quick-swap stacked bottom-right in a tighter arc. Portrait is not the primary design target — levels are authored and tested in landscape first, and portrait is verified as a secondary pass during QA.


## 6. Level Design Philosophy


### 6.1 Guiding Principle: Teach, Test, Twist, Combine

Every world follows a four-beat internal rhythm, regardless of theme:

  1. Teach — a new mechanic or element is introduced in a low-risk space where failure is nearly impossible.
  2. Test — the mechanic is used under mild pressure (simple timing, simple combination with one known element).
  3. Twist — the mechanic is recombined with an earlier one in a way the player hasn't seen, forcing re-application of prior knowledge.
  4. Combine — a capstone level (typically the last 1-2 levels of the world) requires fluent, fast use of everything taught so far.


### 6.2 World Progression

| World | Theme | New Elements / Systems | Design Focus |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| World 1 — Foundations | Basic mechanics | Fire, then Water, then Earth, then Wood introduced one at a time; single-element puzzles only | Teach silhouette recognition, swap timing, and each element's core verb in isolation |
| World 2 — Convergence | Dual-element puzzles | No new elements; introduces puzzles requiring exactly two elements in sequence | Teach sequencing and "undo" thinking (e.g., grow Wood, cross, then burn it behind you) |
| World 3 — Momentum | Timing challenges | Introduces moving hazards, decaying structures, and swap-under-pressure scenarios | Teach that transformation speed itself is a skill, not just a menu choice |
| World 4 — Mastery | Advanced combinations | 3–4 element chains, optional hard-mode variants of earlier mechanics | Capstone world; validates full mastery of the elemental toolkit |


### 6.3 Difficulty Curve

Difficulty is tuned primarily through three levers, in this order of priority: (1) number of elements required in a single puzzle chain, (2) time pressure / hazard movement speed, (3) spatial complexity (level size, number of screens). We deliberately avoid tuning difficulty through punishing input precision — the game should feel hard because of the puzzle logic, not because of tight platforming.

| World | Avg. elements per puzzle | Time pressure | Level length (avg.) | Target fail rate (external testers) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | 1 | None | 45–75s | < 10% |
| 2 | 2 | Low | 60–100s | 10–20% |
| 3 | 2–3 | Moderate | 75–120s | 20–30% |
| 4 | 3–4 | Moderate–High | 90–150s | 25–35% |


### 6.4 Level Length & Pacing Rules

  - No level should require more than 3 minutes for a first-time player who understands the mechanics taught so far.
  - Every level has exactly one required solution path and may have zero or more optional collectible side-paths (see §10).
  - Levels never introduce more than one new concept without a preceding Teach beat.
  - A player should never be more than ~10 seconds from their last checkpoint/attempt start after a failure.


## 7. Puzzle Examples

Concrete, buildable examples an AI coding agent or level designer can use as templates. Each example lists the required elements, the beat it belongs to, and the intended "aha" moment.


### 7.1 Single-Element Puzzles (World 1)

| Puzzle | Element(s) | Beat | Aha Moment |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Burning Poisonous Vines | Fire | Teach | Vines blocking the path glow warm when Fire is active — burn them to clear the route |
| Swimming Through a Flooded Corridor | Water | Teach | A corridor is fully submerged; only Water can traverse it without drowning |
| Pressing a Stone Switch | Earth | Teach | A switch only depresses under Earth's extra weight, opening a gate |
| Growing a Vine Ladder | Wood | Teach | A seed anchor at the base of a tall wall sprouts a climbable vine |


### 7.2 Dual-Element Puzzles (World 2)

| Puzzle | Sequence | Aha Moment |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Wooden Bridge Over Lava | Wood → cross → (bridge remains, no Fire nearby) | Player realizes Wood bridges are permanent unless burned, unlocking route-planning |
| Melt-and-Swim | Fire (melt ice dam) → Water (swim through released flood) | Two elements used back-to-back to transform the level geometry itself |
| Press-and-Push | Earth (press pressure switch to open gate) → Earth (push crate through gate before it re-closes) | Same element used twice for different verbs in one chain |
| Extinguish-to-Cross | Fire (burn vine blocking path) → Water (extinguish a fire barrier further ahead) | Teaches that puzzles are read left-to-right but solved by scanning the whole screen first |


### 7.3 Timing Puzzles (World 3)

| Puzzle | Mechanic | Aha Moment |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Decaying Plank Rush | Wood platform decays in 3s; player must swap to Fire or Earth to interact with what's on the far side before it vanishes | Transformation speed becomes a scoring/survival factor, not just a puzzle key |
| Rising Water Chamber | Water level rises steadily; player alternates Earth (build platform) and Water (swim gap) to stay ahead of it | Forces proactive planning rather than reactive puzzle-solving |
| Timed Fire Gate | A fire switch must be ignited and crossed before a closing gate resets | First real execution-under-pressure test in the game |


### 7.4 Advanced Combination Puzzles (World 4)

| Puzzle | Sequence | Aha Moment |
| --- | --- | --- |
| The Four-Step Vault | Earth (press switch) → Wood (grow bridge) → Fire (ignite far mechanism) → Water (extinguish a resulting fire hazard) → reach goal | Full-kit capstone puzzle; validates total mastery |
| Chain Reaction Causeway | Fire ignites a fuse that collapses an Earth platform, revealing a Water channel, which floods a Wood seed into growth | Introduces multi-stage environmental chain reactions (see §8) |


## 8. Advanced Mechanics


### 8.1 Element Combo Interactions

Combo interactions are pairwise rules that apply consistently across every level, so players can reason about new puzzles using knowledge from old ones. These rules are the puzzle "grammar" of the game.

| Combo | Result | Design Use |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Fire + Wood | Wood structure is destroyed instantly on contact with active flame | Creates "burn the bridge behind you" and "don't bring fire near the vine bridge" puzzles |
| Fire + Earth | Fire can ignite fuses embedded in Earth terrain; Earth itself is fireproof and unaffected otherwise | Long-range trigger puzzles (ignite a fuse that affects a distant Earth mechanism) |
| Fire + Ice (environmental) | Fire melts ice blocks into a temporary water hazard/pool | Chain-reaction puzzles; also a resource — melted water can extinguish other fire hazards |
| Water + Wood | Water causes seed pods to sprout instantly (accelerated growth) rather than requiring Wood's own grow ability | Alternate solution paths; rewards experimentation |
| Water + Fire | Water extinguishes fire hazards and fire-lit fuses on contact | Core "undo" tool; also used defensively against fire traps |
| Water + Electricity (environmental) | Water becomes hazardous while an electrified switch nearby is active | Risk/reward puzzle layer introduced in World 3–4 |
| Earth + Water | Earth platforms submerged in water become slippery (reduced friction) but do not dissolve | Adds light platforming variety without breaking Earth's "solid" identity |
| Earth + Fragile Floor | Earth's weight cracks and eventually breaks marked fragile floors after a short delay | Vertical-descent puzzles; reveals hidden lower routes |


### 8.2 Temporary Environmental Changes

Several interactions leave the level state altered for the remainder of the attempt, which is core to the game's "solve, don't just execute" identity:

  - Melted ice remains water until the level attempt resets (checkpoint respawn does not undo global melts within the same attempt, to avoid punishing exploration).
  - Grown Wood structures persist until burned or until their decay timer elapses (decaying structures are always visually telegraphed at least 2 seconds before disappearing).
  - Broken fragile floors remain broken for the rest of the attempt.
  - Pressure switches held by a pushed crate stay active as long as the crate remains on them, independent of the player's current element.


### 8.3 Optional Collectibles & Hidden Routes

| Collectible Type | Purpose | Typical Placement |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Element Shards | Cosmetic currency; 1–3 per level, always optional | Behind a combo puzzle slightly harder than the level's main path |
| Hidden Route Star | One of the level's 3-star conditions (see §10) | Requires noticing an unlit/unhighlighted interactive object off the critical path |
| Lore Fragments (stretch goal) | Light worldbuilding, fully optional, no gameplay effect | Hidden in dead-end alcoves reachable only via non-obvious combos |


### 8.4 Challenge Objectives

Each level defines up to three optional challenge objectives, surfaced after first completion so they never pressure a first playthrough:

  - Speed target — complete the level under a par time.
  - No-swap-waste — complete the level using at or below a minimum number of element swaps (rewards efficient route planning, not swap-avoidance).
  - Full clear — collect all Element Shards in the level.


## 9. Progression System


### 9.1 Level Unlock System

Levels unlock linearly within a world (Level N unlocks on completion of Level N−1), and worlds unlock on collecting a minimum star threshold from the previous world rather than requiring 100% completion. This keeps skilled players moving forward while still rewarding thoroughness.

| World | Unlock Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| World 1 | Available from game start |
| World 2 | Earn at least 30 of World 1's 45 possible stars (15 levels × 3 stars) |
| World 3 | Earn at least 30 of World 2's 45 possible stars |
| World 4 | Earn at least 30 of World 3's 45 possible stars |


### 9.2 Star Rating System

Each level contains exactly three optional golden Star Collectibles. At level completion, the awarded star rating equals the number of golden Star Collectibles gathered in that attempt (0–3). Each collectible contributes exactly one star; time and swap targets remain separate challenge records and never multiply the star count. Element Shards remain a separate cosmetic currency and do not affect star rating.


### 9.3 Collectibles & Cosmetic Rewards

  - Element Shards accumulate globally and are spent on cosmetic character skins per element (recolors, particle trail variants).
  - Lore Fragments (stretch goal) unlock short flavor-text entries in a Codex menu; purely for engagement, no mechanical effect.
  - A per-world "Master Badge" is awarded for a full 3-star clear of every level in that world.


### 9.4 Replayability

  - Star and challenge-objective chasing gives every level replay value beyond first clear.
  - Daily rotating "Challenge Level" (post-launch, see Future Expansion §16) reuses existing tilesets with new switch/hazard layouts.
  - Leaderboards (optional, requires backend — see Future Expansion) for par-time and minimum-swap records per level.


### 9.5 Difficulty Curve Summary

See §6.3 for the full quantitative curve. At a system level, the curve is enforced by: increasing required elements per puzzle, increasing hazard/timing pressure, and — only as a final lever — increasing level size. This ordering keeps the game feeling "smarter," not "twitchier," as it gets harder.


## 10. Monetization

Monetization is designed around a single hard rule: no ad or payment is ever required to complete a level or progress through the main game. Ads and IAPs only accelerate cosmetic progression or remove friction, never gate content.


### 10.1 Rewarded Ads (Primary Ad Format)

| Placement | Reward | Player Control |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Post-level-fail (3rd consecutive fail on a level) | Optional "Hint" — highlights the next required element for 3 seconds | Fully opt-in via a button; never auto-played |
| Level Select screen | Double Element Shard earnings on next level completion | Opt-in via a button, capped at 3 per day per player to avoid ad fatigue |
| Daily bonus | Small Element Shard bonus for watching one ad per day | Opt-in; visible countdown to next availability |


### 10.2 Optional Interstitial Ads

  - Shown at most once every 3–4 level completions, never on a first attempt at a level and never immediately after a fail (to avoid punishing frustration with an ad).
  - Fully removable via the one-time IAP described in 10.3.
  - A frequency cap and cooldown are configured server-side (remote config) so they can be tuned post-launch without a client update.


### 10.3 Premium Upgrade

| Item | Price Band (indicative) | Contents |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Remove Ads | $2.99–$3.99 | Removes all interstitial ads permanently; rewarded ads remain available as an opt-in choice since players may still want the bonus rewards |
| Remove Ads + Skin Bundle | $5.99–$7.99 | Remove Ads plus 4 exclusive cosmetic element skins (one per element), no gameplay effect |


### 10.4 Cosmetics

Cosmetic skins recolor the character's active-element silhouette and particle trail. They are purely visual, purchasable with earned Element Shards or unlocked via the premium bundle, and never affect hitboxes, ability behavior, or difficulty — preserving the "no pay-to-win" principle.


### 10.5 Explicit Non-Goals

  - No energy/lives system that gates play by a timer.
  - No paid difficulty skips.
  - No loot boxes or randomized paid rewards.
  - No ads during active gameplay (mid-level).


## 11. Audio Design


### 11.1 Element Transformation Sounds

| Element | Sound Character | Duration |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Fire | Sharp whoosh + crackle burst | < 300ms |
| Water | Splash droplet + soft ring resonance | < 300ms |
| Earth | Low thud + gravel shift | < 300ms |
| Wood | Organic creak + soft chime (growth motif) | < 300ms |

All four stingers share a common rhythmic "shape" (a fast attack, short decay) so they read as one cohesive transformation system rather than four unrelated sound effects — this reinforces Pillar 1.


### 11.2 Ambient Sounds

  - Each world has a distinct ambient bed (World 1: gentle wind and birdsong; World 2: distant water flow; World 3: rhythmic mechanical ticking to underscore timing; World 4: layered, subtly tenser ambience combining prior motifs).
  - Ambient audio ducks (reduces volume) briefly during transformation stingers and key puzzle-solve moments so feedback is never masked.


### 11.3 Puzzle Feedback Sounds

| Event | Sound |
| --- | --- |
| Switch activated | Bright confirmation chime, pitched by switch type (fire/water/earth/universal) |
| Structure decay warning | Rising tick/pulse starting 2s before decay |
| Hazard death | Soft, non-punishing "poof" — never harsh or frustrating, to keep failure low-stakes |
| Level complete | Short triumphant fanfare, 1.5–2s, non-looping |
| Collectible pickup | Light sparkle chime, pitch increases slightly with each shard collected in sequence |


### 11.4 UI Sounds

  - Minimal, soft "tap" confirmation for menu buttons.
  - Distinct, slightly warmer tone for star-earned and level-unlock moments.
  - All UI sounds respect a separate UI-volume slider independent of SFX and Music sliders.


### 11.5 Music Direction

Light, melodic, loopable tracks with a modern casual-mobile character (think mallet percussion, plucked strings, soft synth pads) — upbeat but not distracting, since puzzles require focus. Each world's music layers in the element motifs established in the transformation stingers, so the score subtly reinforces which elements a world is teaching. Music tempo increases modestly (not tempo-syncing gameplay, just mood) from World 1 to World 4 to track rising tension.


## 12. Visual Identity


### 12.1 Color Palette

| Palette Role | Colors | Usage |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Element core colors | Fire #C0392B, Water #1F6FB2, Earth #8A5A32, Wood #3D7A3D | Character form, UI buttons, switches, hazards |
| Element highlight colors | Fire #FF8A50, Water #7FD1E8, Earth #D9A15B, Wood #8FD17A | Particles, glows, interactive-object highlights |
| Neutral environment palette | Soft creams, pale greys, muted pastels | Background/terrain, kept low-saturation so element colors pop |
| UI accent | #5B3E96 (soft violet) | HUD chrome, buttons, menus — deliberately outside the four element hues to avoid confusion |


### 12.2 Character Silhouettes

Each element form shares the same base proportions (so the player never has to relearn hitbox size) but has a distinct silhouette treatment: Fire has flame-tipped extremities and a flickering outline; Water has a rounded, flowing outline with a translucent lower body; Earth is blockier and wider with visible rock-plate seams; Wood has slightly elongated limbs with leaf/vine accents at the joints. This ensures element identity is readable even in grayscale or by colorblind players.


### 12.3 Particle Effects

  - Transformation burst: radial particle ring in the new element's highlight color, 12–16 particles, 0.3s lifetime.
  - Ambient idle particles per element (embers for Fire, droplets for Water, dust motes for Earth, floating leaves for Wood) at low density so they read as flavor, not noise.
  - Interaction particles (e.g., vine burning, ice melting) are capped at a budget of 40 concurrent particles per effect to protect frame rate on mid-tier devices.


### 12.4 Animation Priorities

  1. Transformation morph (highest priority — this is the core fantasy).
  2. Idle and run cycles per element (distinct enough to read at a glance).
  3. Ability-use animations (burn, swim stroke, push, grow) — must clearly telegraph effect timing so puzzles feel fair.
  4. Death/respawn — quick and low-stakes, under 0.5s.
  5. UI/menu transitions — lowest priority, can reuse simple fades/slides.


### 12.5 Readability on Small Screens

  - Minimum on-screen character size tested at 5.5" device reference resolution; silhouette must remain distinguishable at that scale.
  - Interactive objects always carry a secondary non-color cue (icon or shape) in addition to color-coding.
  - HUD elements maintain a minimum 44×44dp touch target per platform mobile HCI guidelines, with generous spacing to avoid accidental multi-touch on the swap cluster.
  - Background contrast is kept deliberately low relative to foreground interactive elements, following a consistent "foreground = saturated, background = muted" rule across all worlds.



