## 16. Scene Management

Scene/screen flow is split across two layers: Flutter's router handles non-game screens (menus), while Flame's own overlay/scene system handles in-game states (playing, paused, level-complete) so the game loop doesn't have to tear down and rebuild the GameWidget for every UI state.


### 16.1 Flutter-Level Navigation (Screens)

```
SplashScreen → HomeScreen ─┬─→ WorldSelectScreen → LevelSelectScreen → GameScreen(levelId)
                           ├─→ SettingsScreen
                           ├─→ StoreScreen
                           └─→ CodexScreen (stretch)

GameScreen hosts a single persistent GameWidget<ElementShiftGame> for the session;
leaving GameScreen disposes the current LevelWorld but can keep ElementShiftGame warm
if the player is likely to return (e.g., replaying via 'Next Level').
```


### 16.2 Flame-Level Scene States

Within a single GameScreen session, Flame uses overlays (Flutter widgets drawn above the Canvas) plus a lightweight internal `GameSceneState` enum, rather than swapping FlameGame instances, to keep transitions instant:

| State | Description | Overlay(s) Shown |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Loading | LevelLoader parsing JSON and instantiating LevelWorld | LoadingOverlay (spinner/element icon animation) |
| Playing | Normal gameplay; input active | HUD (movement, jump, swap cluster, pause button) |
| Paused | Game loop paused (update() early-returns); input redirected to pause menu | PauseOverlay |
| LevelComplete | Objective results computed, entities frozen | LevelCompleteOverlay (stars, shard count, next/retry buttons) |
| LevelFailed (rare, only for optional fail states) | Most 'failure' is just respawn-to-checkpoint, not a full scene state — reserved for edge cases like intentional soft-lock detection | RespawnOverlay (brief, auto-dismisses) |


### 16.3 Scene Flow Diagram

```
