## 17. State Management

State is split into three tiers with different lifetimes and different tools, to avoid the common mobile-game mistake of one giant global game state object.

| Tier | Examples | Lifetime | Tool |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| App state (Flutter shell) | Settings, unlocked worlds, owned cosmetics, Element Shard balance | Persisted across sessions | Riverpod providers backed by SaveService |
| Session state (per game session) | Current level, current attempt number, HUD visibility, pause state | Lives for the GameScreen session | Riverpod (ephemeral providers) + ElementShiftGame fields |
| Level-runtime state (per attempt) | Player position, current element, entity positions, objective progress, checkpoint reached | Reset on retry/respawn | Owned by LevelWorld and its child components |


### 17.1 Player Element State Machine

The player's element is modeled as an explicit finite state machine rather than a raw enum field, so swap validity, transition events, and edge cases (e.g., swap requested mid-death-animation) are handled in one place.

```
