## 20. Audio Management

An `AudioManager` singleton-style service (wrapping flame_audio) owns three independent channels — Music, SFX, and UI — each with its own volume control, plus a lightweight priority/pooling system for SFX so rapid element swaps never cause audio glitching or channel exhaustion.


### 20.1 Responsibilities

| Responsibility | Detail |
| --- | --- |
| Channel volume control | Independent Music/SFX/UI sliders, persisted via Settings (see §18.1) |
| SFX pooling | Pre-loads and pools the 4 transformation stingers + common puzzle-feedback sounds at scene load to avoid first-play latency; caps concurrent SFX instances (e.g., 8) with oldest-instance eviction |
| Music crossfade | 0.5–1s crossfade between world themes on level transition; ducking (see §11.2) on key feedback moments |
| Haptics coordination | Triggers device haptic feedback in sync with transformation and hazard-death SFX, gated by the Settings haptics toggle |


### 20.2 Event-Driven Playback

Gameplay systems never call audio playback APIs directly; they emit semantic events (e.g., `ElementTransformed(to: ElementType.water)`, `SwitchActivated(type: SwitchType.fire)`) that AudioManager subscribes to and maps to specific sound assets. This indirection means sound design can be iterated (remapped, remixed) without touching gameplay code — an important property for AI-agent-driven iteration.


## 21. Animation Management


### 21.1 Animation Data Model

Each element defines a fixed animation state set so the PlayerVisualComponent can be driven generically rather than with per-element special-case code:

  - idle, run, jump, fall — locomotion states, looped or single-shot as appropriate
  - ability — plays while an element-specific ability is active (e.g., Wood's grow, Fire's ignite)
  - transformIn — plays for the fixed 0.2–0.3s swap window when this element becomes active
  - death — short, non-looping, triggers respawn on completion


### 21.2 Animation Component Contract

`PlayerVisualComponent` exposes a small state-driven API consumed by ElementStateMachine and PlayerPhysicsComponent: `setElement(ElementType)`, `playLocomotion(LocomotionState)`, `playAbility(AbilityId)`, `playTransformIn(from: ElementType, to: ElementType)`. It internally resolves these calls to the correct SpriteAnimation from the current element's pre-loaded animation set, so no other system needs to know asset file names.


### 21.3 Performance Approach

  - All four elements' spritesheets for the currently loaded level's required elements are pre-loaded at level-load time (Loading scene state) to guarantee the transformation animation never stalls on a texture load.
  - Sprite atlases are used (not per-frame image files) to minimize draw calls and texture swaps, which matters most during rapid swap sequences.
  - Particle-based FX (transformation burst, ability effects) are pooled objects, not instantiated/destroyed per use, to avoid GC pressure on mid-tier Android devices.


## 22. Collision Handling


### 22.1 Approach

Element Shift uses Flame's built-in hitbox/collision detection system (`CollisionCallbacks`, `RectangleHitbox`/`PolygonHitbox`) rather than a full physics engine, since the game does not require rigid-body dynamics — only axis-aligned platforming collision, trigger volumes, and simple pushable-object movement. This keeps behavior predictable and easy to tune, which matters for puzzle fairness.


### 22.2 Collision Categories

| Category | Examples | Response |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Solid Terrain | Ground, walls, platforms | Standard block collision (stop movement, ground the player) |
| One-Way Platform | Jump-through platforms | Collision only from above while falling |
| Hazard (element-aware) | Fire pool, water hazard, electric zone | Trigger volume; queries player's current ElementType to determine harmless-pass-through vs. damage/respawn |
| Interactable Trigger | Switches, seed anchors, crates | Trigger volume; dispatches an interaction event consumed by the relevant systems.dart handler |
| Structure (runtime-spawned) | Wood bridge, Earth platform | Solid terrain once active; removed from collision world on decay/destruction |
| Goal | Level exit | Trigger volume; fires LevelCompleted flow |


### 22.3 Element-Aware Collision Resolution

A single `HazardResponseResolver` centralizes the "which element survives which hazard" logic (mirroring the Element Comparison Matrix in §3.5 and the Combo table in §8.1), rather than scattering `if (element == fire)` checks across many hazard classes. This is the single place the design team (or an AI agent) edits to rebalance element interactions.

```
HazardResponseResolver.resolve(hazardType, currentElement) → HazardOutcome

  {FireHazard,  Fire}   → Outcome.passThrough
  {FireHazard,  Water}  → Outcome.extinguishesHazard   // water puts out fire
  {FireHazard,  Earth}  → Outcome.respawn
  {FireHazard,  Wood}   → Outcome.respawn               // wood burns
  {WaterHazard, Water}  → Outcome.passThrough (swim)
  {WaterHazard, Fire}   → Outcome.respawn (extinguished)
  {WaterHazard, Earth}  → Outcome.sinks → Outcome.respawn
  {WaterHazard, Wood}   → Outcome.floats / context-specific
  {ElectricHazard, Water} → Outcome.respawn (only when hazard is 'live')
  {CrumblingFloor, Earth} → Outcome.delayedCollapse(seconds: 1.5)
```


### 22.4 Collision Performance

  - Broad-phase spatial partitioning (Flame's built-in QuadTree/Sweep support) is enabled once a level exceeds ~40 collidable entities, to keep collision checks sub-linear.
  - Hazard/interactable trigger checks run at fixed update-step cadence (not per-render-frame) to decouple puzzle logic from variable frame rate.


## 23. Level Loading Strategy


### 23.1 Level Data Format

Levels are authored in the Tiled map editor (.tmx) and exported to Tiled's JSON format, then validated against an internal schema before being committed to `assets/levels/`. This gives designers a visual authoring tool while keeping the runtime format simple, versioned, and diffable.

```
{
  "levelId": "level_w1_07",
  "world": 1,
  "index": 7,
  "gridSize": { "cols": 40, "rows": 12, "tileSize": 32 },
  "requiredElements": ["fire", "water"],
  "parTimeMs": 45000,
  "minSwapTarget": 6,
  "playerStart": { "x": 64, "y": 320 },
  "checkpoints": [ { "x": 640, "y": 320 } ],
  "goal": { "x": 1200, "y": 288 },
  "terrain": [ /* tile layer references */ ],
  "hazards": [ { "type": "fireHazard", "x": 384, "y": 320, "w": 96, "h": 32 } ],
  "interactables": [ { "type": "seedAnchor", "id": "seed_1", "x": 512, "y": 288 } ],
  "shards": [ { "x": 700, "y": 250 }, { "x": 900, "y": 200 } ]
}
```

[Figure 23.1 — Simplified level JSON schema (illustrative subset).]


### 23.2 Loading Sequence

  1. Flutter's GameScreen receives a `levelId` (from LevelSelectScreen navigation).
  2. A `LoadLevel(levelId)` command is sent across the event bus to the running ElementShiftGame.
  3. SceneRouter transitions to the Loading state; the previous LevelWorld (if any) is disposed.
  4. LevelLoader fetches the level's JSON from assets, validates it against the schema, and resolves `requiredElements` to determine which element spritesheets/animations to pre-load.
  5. LevelLoader constructs a new LevelWorld component tree: terrain, hazards, interactables, checkpoints, goal, and the PlayerComponent at `playerStart`.
  6. Once all required assets report loaded, SceneRouter transitions to Playing and input is enabled.


### 23.3 Streaming & Memory Strategy

  - Only the current level's required-element assets and the current world's tileset are kept resident; other worlds' assets are released on world transition (Flutter's asset cache eviction, or manual disposal for large atlases).
  - World 1 assets ship in the base install; Worlds 2–4 (and any post-launch worlds) are delivered as on-demand asset packs via Play Asset Delivery on Android, with a graceful "downloading world" loading state.
  - Level JSON files are tiny (a few KB each) and can all ship in the base install regardless of which world's art has been downloaded, so level-select screens can show full world structure even before art assets are fetched.


### 23.4 Retry/Respawn Path (Fast Path)

Respawning to the last checkpoint does not re-run the full Loading sequence — it is a lightweight in-place reset handled entirely within the already-loaded LevelWorld (reset player position/element to checkpoint state, reset any non-persistent hazard state, keep persistent-within-attempt changes like melted ice per §8.2). This keeps the ~10-second-to-retry promise from §6.4 realistic.



