## 29. Per-Level Authoring Checklist

Every level, whether built by a human designer or generated/assisted by an AI agent from this framework, must satisfy this checklist before being marked ready for playtesting:

  1. Has exactly one required critical-path solution (verified by a solver pass or manual walkthrough).
  2. Requires no more than one new concept beyond what prior levels in the world have taught (see §6.1 Teach/Test/Twist/Combine).
  3. Completable within the target length for its world (see §6.3 table).
  4. Defines exactly three golden Star Collectibles for the 0–3 star rating; parTimeMs or minSwapTarget may be configured separately as an optional challenge objective.
  5. Places 0–3 Element Shards, each behind a path slightly harder than the critical path (never on the critical path itself).
  6. Every hazard and interactable uses only the approved visual/audio language from §3 and §11.3 — no bespoke unexplained mechanics.
  7. Checkpoint(s) placed so no failure requires more than ~10 seconds of backtracking (§6.4).
  8. Validated against the Element Comparison Matrix (§3.5) and Combo table (§8.1) — no interaction contradicts established rules.
  9. Tagged with `requiredElements` in its level JSON (§23.1) so LevelLoader pre-loads only what's needed.


### 29.1 Level JSON Authoring Reference

See §23.1, Figure 23.1 for the canonical level schema. Designers and AI agents should author new levels against that schema directly, or via the Tiled editor export pipeline (§19.3), rather than hand-writing JSON for complex levels.


## 30. Difficulty & Pacing — Production Targets

Restating §6.3 as concrete production targets for QA sign-off:

| Metric | World 1 | World 2 | World 3 | World 4 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Target external-tester fail rate | < 10% | 10–20% | 20–30% | 25–35% |
| Avg. elements required per puzzle | 1 | 2 | 2–3 | 3–4 |
| Avg. level completion time (first attempt) | 45–75s | 60–100s | 75–120s | 90–150s |
| Max acceptable session-ending frustration events (per playtest) | 0 | ≤ 1 | ≤ 2 | ≤ 2 |

"Frustration events" are defined during playtesting as any moment a tester expresses that a failure felt unfair (as opposed to a fair puzzle-logic mistake) — these are tracked qualitatively during moderated playtests in the Alpha/Beta milestones (Part V).



