## 34. Guardrails for AI Coding Agents

These constraints exist to prevent an AI agent from quietly drifting away from the architecture defined in Part II while iterating quickly.

  - Never hard-code level content in Dart classes — all level content flows through the LevelLoader/JSON pipeline (§23).
  - Never add direct storage/IO calls inside Flame-layer classes — persistence always flows through SaveService via the event bus (§17.2, §18.2).
  - Never special-case an element inside a hazard or interactable class — element-vs-hazard outcomes belong in HazardResponseResolver (§22.3) so the rules stay centralized and auditable.
  - Never introduce a new cross-boundary Flutter/Flame call without adding it to the Event Bus table (§17.2) first — undocumented coupling is the fastest way to lose consistency across AI-assisted sessions.
  - When a task is ambiguous, prefer the simplest implementation consistent with the referenced GDD/SDD section over inventing new systems; flag genuine gaps for a design-doc update rather than silently improvising a new mechanic.
  - Keep puzzle/interaction logic (what happens) decoupled from rendering/animation code (how it looks) per §13.1, so logic remains unit-testable without booting Flame.



